COE\nMajor Requirements: GSM\nInternational Admission\nInternational Admission\nInternational Exam and Visa Requirements\nInternational Records\nInternational Student Resources\nAfter You Apply\nAfter You Apply\nDeferred Enrollment\nReporting Changes After Admission\nApplicant Waitlist\nAdmitted First-Years\nAdmitted Transfer Students\nApply to UC Davis\nApply to UC Davis\nApplication Review Criteria\nPersonal Insight Questions\nEvents\nEvents\nDiscover UC Davis\nAggie Transfer Scholars Weekend\nAggie to Be\nContact Undergraduate Admissions\nParents and Families\nCounselors\nHow to Get into UC Davis\nMore Information\nGraduate Admissions\nProfessional Admissions\nCost and Aid\nCost and Aid\nFinancial Aid and Scholarships\nHigher Education Emergency Relief Fund\nNext Steps\nEducational Opportunity Program\nUndocumented Student Applicants\nApply to UC Davis\nAcademics\nAcademics\nMajors\nMajors\nMajors Blog\nMinors\nGraduate Programs\nGraduate Programs\nGraduate Studies\nProfessional Programs\nColleges and Schools\nColleges and Schools\nWhether you are an undergraduate, graduate or professional student, you’ll find our colleges and schools connect you to peers, staff and faculty dedicated to your success.\nSchool of Law\nSchool of Medicine\nSchool of Nursing\nSchool of Education\nSchool of Veterinary Medicine\nCollege of Agricultural and Environmental Science\nCollege of Biological Sciences\nCollege of Engineering\nCollege of Letters and Science\nGraduate School of Management\nOur Faculty\nStudy Abroad\nResearch\nResearch\nFrom Labs to Lives\nUndergraduate Research\nGraduate Research\nOffice of Research\nLibrary\nMuseums and Collections\nResearch Partners\nUC Davis Discoveries\nUC Davis Discoveries\nAgricultural Discoveries\nAgricultural Discoveries\nBlazing the Way for Chardonnay\nGrowing Better Strawberries\nBreeding Better Bees\nMaking Tougher Tomatoes\nHacking Hardier Rice\nBiology Discoveries\nBiology Discoveries\nChloroplasts Contain DNA\nTardigrades: Extreme Survival\nEngineering Discoveries\nEngineering Discoveries\nBroadband Communications\nGiving People Their Voice\nPlug-In Hybrid Cars\nEnvironmental Discoveries\nEnvironmental Discoveries\nGetting Lead Out of Gasoline\nSaving Lake Tahoe\nMedical Discoveries\nMedical Discoveries\nIdentifying Feline AIDS\nFragile X Syndrome (FXTAS)B\nWhole-Body PET Scan\nBetter HIV Treatment\nAccelerating Cancer-Fighting Drugs\nImaging Unwinding DNA\nThe One Health Model\nSocial Science Discoveries\nSocial Science Discoveries\nBaby Sign Language\nEarly Hunters Were Female\nTransforming Attachment Theory\nVeterinary Science Discoveries\nVeterinary Science Discoveries\nTreating Animal Burns With Tilapia Skins\nCampus Life\nCampus Life\nCalifornia’s College Town\nCalifornia’s College Town\nThe College Tour\nThings To Do\nThings To Do\nTraditions\nTraditions\n#ourUCDavis\nFall Welcome\nHomecoming\nPicnic Day\nArboretum\nArts and Culture\nCalendar\nCalendar\nCampus Poster Calendars\nHousing\nDining\nStudent Jobs\nClubs and Communities\nAthletics\nNews\nNews\nLatest News\nIn Focus Features\nUniversity News\nDateline\nUC Davis Magazine\nSocial Media\nSocial Media\nSocial Media Guidelines\nStudent News\nBlogs\nPodcasts and Shows\nPodcasts and Shows\nFace to Face\nThat May Be the Chancellor\nThursday Thoughts\nUnfold\nThe Backdrop\nUC Davis LIVE\nSearch\nUC Davis\nIn Focus\nMagazine\nPodcasts and Shows\nDateline\nMedia Resources\nExperts\nAdvancing Health Worldwide\nRelated Articles\nHow Does the Human Brain Help Us Navigate?\nFederal Funding Protects California’s Future\nValley Fever Is Spreading Across the Arid West, Affecting People and Dogs\nMore in Advancing Health Worldwide\nJakiro or \"Jak,\" a 9-year-old black domestic shorthair owned by Tina Thomas sleeps in the sun. Jak was part of a clinical trial testing a new cancer drug for cats with squamous cell carcinoma. He survived more than eight months after taking part in the clinical trial. (Tina Thomas)\nNew Cancer Drug Could Help Cats and People\nFirst Clinical Trial Finds Disease Controlled in One-Third of Cats\nby\nAmy Quinton\nAugust 28, 2025\nScientists at the University of California, San Francisco and the University of California, Davis, have completed the first clinical trial of a new cancer drug in pet cats, offering hope for a disease that has long been nearly impossible to treat. The\nstudy\nfound 35 percent of the cats with squamous cell carcinoma who received treatment had their disease controlled with minimal side effects. The drug will likely be effective for humans with head and neck squamous cell carcinoma. The\nstudy\nwas published today in Cancer Cell.\nOral squamous cell carcinoma is one of the most aggressive cancers in cats. Most cats survive only two to three months after diagnosis, since surgery, chemotherapy and radiation usually do little to stop it.\n“One of the struggles I’ve had my entire career is that cats with this cancer—there is really nothing we can do,” said second author Katherine Skorupski, a professor of clinical medical oncology with the UC Davis School of Veterinary Medicine. “We see these cats, we give the bad news, and we do our best to keep them comfortable until they are euthanized. So the fact that we would have something, anything, that might help these cats is so exciting.”\nShe said in the future, the drug will likely be used with other therapies. Cats, who often don’t get much attention in cancer research, could benefit greatly.\nThe drug, which was originally designed to treat human head and neck cancers, is the first of its kind to target a protein called STAT3. This protein acts like a faulty switch that tells cancer cells to keep growing.\n“What’s exciting is that this study shows it’s possible to shut down a protein that drives cancer,” said first author Daniel Johnson with the University of California, San Francisco Helen Diller Family Comprehensive Cancer Center. “That has been notoriously difficult in the past.” He added that the study also shows that pets with cancer can be good models of human disease and they may yield more reliable results than mouse models.\nThe idea to test the drug in cats came from a discussion first author Jennifer Grandis, with UCSF, had with her sister, a veterinarian. She learned that oral cancers are extremely difficult to treat. The UCSF researchers coordinated with veterinarians at UC Davis to recruit pets for the trial.\nMore time with family\nOne cat who benefitted from the trial was Jakiro, or “Jak,” a 9-year-old black shorthair. His owner, Tina Thomas, was devastated when told he had only 6 to 8 weeks to live.\n“It was just a gut punch,” she said. “We wanted more time with him. When I found out about this clinical trial, I knew I wanted him to be a part of it.”\nJakiro or \"Jak\" was part of the clinical trial. He went on to live an additional eight months. (Tina Thomas)\nJak received weekly treatments for a month. His symptoms improved, and he went on to live more than eight months—long enough to spend another Christmas with his family.\n“It was invaluable to us to give him time where he was comfortable and could enjoy being around his family,” Thomas said. “He loved being under our Christmas tree and giving him that one more Christmas, which I don’t think he would have had otherwise, just made us feel good.”\nCats as models\nOther than mild anemia, none of the cats in the trial developed side effects that were attributable to the treatment. Of the 20 cats that were enrolled, 7 of them exhibited either a partial response or stable disease during the study period. Among the 7 that responded, the average survival post-treatment was 161 days.\nImportantly, the treatment appeared to not only block the activity of STAT3 but also raise levels of a protein associated with an immune response to cancer.\n“By partnering with veterinary oncologists and doing clinical trials in companion animals, we can learn an enormous amount about how these drugs work while also helping people’s pets. None of the cats in these trials were harmed, and many of them benefited,” Grandis said.\nResearchers are currently working with a small biotech company to advance the new compound in clinical trials for both pets and humans.\nOther UC Davis authors include Robert B. Rebhun, Daniel York, Hong Chang, and Ellen E. Sparger.\nThis work was supported by the National Institutes of Health and the Center for Companion Animal Health at UC Davis.\nMedia Resources\nMedia Contacts:\nDaniel Johnson\n,\nUCSF\n,\nDaniel.Johnson@ucsf.edu\nJennifer Grandis\n,\nUCSF\n,\nJennifer.Grandis@ucsf.edu\nKatherine Skorupski, UC Davis School of Veterinary Medicine,\nkskorups@ucdavis.edu\nElizabeth Fernandez, UCSF News and Media Relations,\nElizabeth.Fernandez@UCSF.edu\n415-502-6397\nAmy Quinton, UC Davis News and Media Relations\namquinton@ucdavis.edu\n530-601-8077\nTags\nCats\nCancer\nSchool of Veterinary Medicine\nVeterinary Medical Teaching Hospital\nTrending\nCancer Research\nSchools\nBetty Irene Moore School of Nursing\nContinuing and Professional Education\nGraduate School of Management\nSchool of Education\nSchool of Law\nSchool of Medicine\nSchool of Veterinary Medicine\nColleges\nAgricultural and Environmental Sciences\nBiological Sciences\nEngineering\nLetters and Science\nResearch Centers\nAggie Square\nBodega Marine Laboratory\nComprehensive Cancer Center\nMcClellan Nuclear Research Center\nNational Biomedical Research Institute\nRobert Mondavi Institute for Wine and Food Science\nTahoe Environmental Research Center\nUC Davis Institute of the Environment\nCampus\nCampus Map\nDirections and Parking\nEmergency Services\nHealthcare\nStudent Health and Counseling Services\nUC Davis Health\nVeterinary Medicine\nResources\nAcademic Calendar\nDirectory\nApplication Information\nCourse Schedule\nJobs\nOne Shields Avenue\nDavis, CA 96158\n530-752-1011\nContact Us\nMake a Gift\nUniversity of California, Davis\n, One Shields Avenue, Davis, CA 95616 | 530-752-1011\nQuestions or comme